<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Ba</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 316 BCE (approx.) · capital Jiangzhou</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of the Ba warriors in the Three Gorges and Chongqing region, famed for salt springs and war dances, conquered by Qin with its neighbour Shu in 316 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Ba were peoples of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> basin and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-gorges">Three Gorges</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>. Tradition says their warriors fought alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wu-of-zhou">King Wu of Zhou</a> against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang</a>, singing and dancing as they advanced. A state led by a royal Ba lineage took shape in the 1st millennium BCE, its centre eventually at Jiangzhou in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ba controlled valuable brine springs and salt production along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, which gave it wealth and influence over trade with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu</a>. Its elite were buried in boat-shaped coffins and used distinctive willow-leaf bronze swords marked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ba-shu-script">Ba-Shu symbols</a> that remain undeciphered. Relations with Chu alternated between alliance and war.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Fighting between Ba and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ancient-shu">Ancient Shu</a> gave the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> its opportunity: in 316 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> armies conquered both and captured the king of Ba. Qin made the area Ba <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">Commandery</a>, while Ba elites kept local privileges. The name survives in the traditional term <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ba-shu-script">Ba-Shu</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> culture.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-ba.jpg" alt="State of Ba" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:States_of_Zhou_Dynasty.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 316 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jiangzhou</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ba local religion</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ba</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-658&amp;focus=state-of-ba" aria-label="State of Ba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7521 1072 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Ba lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7841 1328L7768 1187"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7768 1187h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7841 1328h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7841 1328h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=-658&amp;focus=state-of-ba">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

